import Sql from '../../components/explainers/querying/Sql.astro';
import PlanFigure from '../../components/relational-next/a26/PlanFigure.astro';
import worksheet from '../../../docs/article-work/relational-writing-2026-10-19/nodes/a26/evidence/verify.mjs?url&no-inline';
const sections=[['collect-evidence-for-the-slow-report-safely','Collect evidence for the slow report safely'],['follow-where-the-report-spends-its-work','Follow where the report spends its work'],['interpret-counts-costs-and-timings-before-blaming-a-node','Interpret counts, costs and timings before blaming a node'],['locate-the-first-error-that-could-change-the-choice','Locate the first error that could change the choice'],['check-the-statistics-behind-that-prediction','Check the statistics behind that prediction'],['check-the-application-s-common-and-rare-parameters',"Check the application’s common and rare parameters"],['verify-the-remedy-and-close-the-investigation','Verify the remedy and close the investigation']];
const report=`SELECT c.customer_id,
       sum(l.quantity * l.unit_price) AS spent
FROM customers c
JOIN orders o USING (customer_id)
JOIN order_lines l USING (order_id)
WHERE c.region = $1
  AND o.placed_at >= DATE '2026-01-01'
  AND o.placed_at <  DATE '2027-01-01'
GROUP BY c.customer_id
ORDER BY spent DESC NULLS LAST, c.customer_id
LIMIT 10;`;
const schema=`CREATE TABLE customers (
  customer_id integer PRIMARY KEY,
  region text, country text
);
CREATE TABLE orders (
  order_id integer PRIMARY KEY,
  customer_id integer NOT NULL REFERENCES customers,
  placed_at date NOT NULL,
  revision integer NOT NULL DEFAULT 0
);
CREATE TABLE order_lines (
  order_id integer REFERENCES orders,
  line_no integer,
  quantity integer NOT NULL,
  unit_price numeric(10,2),
  PRIMARY KEY (order_id, line_no)
);
CREATE INDEX orders_customer ON orders (customer_id);`;
const before=`Limit                          estimated rows=10   actual rows=10 loops=1
  Sort: spent DESC NULLS LAST, customer_id; top-N heapsort, 25 kB
    HashAggregate              estimated rows=18   actual rows=8000 loops=1
      Nested Loop              estimated rows=303  actual rows=240000 loops=1
        Nested Loop            estimated rows=101  actual rows=80000 loops=1
          Seq Scan customers   estimated rows=18   actual rows=8000 loops=1
            Filter: region = 'north'; 2000 rows removed
          Bitmap Heap Scan orders
                               estimated rows=10   actual rows=10 loops=8000
            Recheck Cond: customer_id = c.customer_id
            Filter: placed_at >= '2026-01-01' AND placed_at < '2027-01-01'
            Bitmap Index Scan orders_customer
              Index Cond: customer_id = c.customer_id
        Index Scan order_lines_pkey
                               estimated rows=3    actual rows=3 loops=80000
          Index Cond: order_id = o.order_id`;
const node=`Index Scan using order_lines_pkey on order_lines l
  (cost=0.42..0.60 rows=3 width=13)
  (actual time=0.001..0.001 rows=3.00 loops=80000)
  Index Cond: (order_id = o.order_id)
  Index Searches: 80000
  Buffers: shared hit=480000`;
const stats=`SELECT reltuples, relpages
FROM pg_class WHERE oid = 'customers'::regclass;

SELECT attname, null_frac, n_distinct,
       most_common_vals, most_common_freqs,
       histogram_bounds, correlation
FROM pg_stats
WHERE schemaname = 'public' AND tablename = 'customers';

-- This full count is bounded on the disposable 10,000-row fixture.
SELECT region, country, count(*)
FROM customers GROUP BY region, country;

ANALYZE customers;`;
const prepared=`-- After PREPARE diagnostic_report(text) AS <the same report>:
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, TIMING OFF, SETTINGS)
EXECUTE diagnostic_report('north');

SELECT name, generic_plans, custom_plans
FROM pg_prepared_statements;

-- Optional isolated comparison, inside one test transaction:
BEGIN;
SET LOCAL plan_cache_mode = force_generic_plan;
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, TIMING OFF)
EXECUTE diagnostic_report('island');
ROLLBACK;`;
---
<p>A spending report returns ten customers but performs 80,000 line-item lookups to decide which ten they are. Its plan predicts only a few hundred joined rows. The useful question is where that prediction went wrong, and whether correcting it removes enough work to help the application.</p>
<p>This article completes that investigation on PostgreSQL 18.6: collect evidence, follow the work, test a statistics refresh and check the application’s common and rare parameters. First finish <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a>, including <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/#change-one-explanation-into-a-measured-improvement">its comparison method</a>. We use that method here, with more detail about what a plan can establish.</p>
<nav aria-label="In this article"><ol>{sections.map(([id,title])=><li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<h2 id="collect-evidence-for-the-slow-report-safely">Collect evidence for the slow report safely</h2>
<p>The shop ranks customers by their line-item spending during a dated interval. The region is a text parameter. <code>north</code> is common, with 8,000 of 10,000 customers; <code>island</code> is rare, with ten. Each customer has ten orders, and each order three lines. All 100,000 orders fall within the interval. The regular data makes repeated work easy to count.</p>
<Sql code={report} label="One report, with region supplied as a parameter" />
<p>The answer has one row per customer, and every qualifying line contributes to its sum. Separate lines with equal values still contribute separately. <code>numeric</code> prices keep exact decimal arithmetic. A null price contributes no value to <code>sum</code>; an all-null group has a null total and sorts last. Customer identifier breaks tied totals deterministically. Customers without a matching order and line are excluded by the inner joins.</p>
<details><summary>Schema and capture conditions</summary><Sql code={schema} label="The tables and existing indexes; no candidate index is added" /><p>The worksheet disables automatic vacuum and analysis only on these disposable tables, so the statistics transition remains observable. It first collects customer statistics with ten north customers, then changes another 7,990 to north. There are also 1,990 south customers. Country is a dependent attribute used later to explain statistics limits; it is absent from the report predicate.</p><p>Captured server: native PostgreSQL 18.6, 8 KiB blocks, 32 MB shared buffers, 4 MB work memory, default statistics target 100. Sessions use UTC, no parallel workers and JIT off, with 15-second statement and two-second lock timeouts. I/O timing is off. No server restart or cache flush is used. The retained worksheet contains the generator, exact settings and full JSON plans.</p></details>
<p><code>EXPLAIN</code> plans without executing the statement. <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, TIMING OFF)</code> executes and reports observed rows and buffers, with overall execution time. Here <code>ANALYZE</code> is an execution option; the separate command <code>ANALYZE customers</code> collects planner statistics. PostgreSQL 18 enables buffer reporting with execution analysis by default; writing <code>BUFFERS</code> makes the intention explicit.</p>
<p>Execution evidence carries the statement’s effects and cost. It can write data, call effectful functions or triggers, take locks and compete with other requests. A rollback does not undo sequence advances or external work. Start on disposable data; use <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">service operations</a> for appropriately scoped live collection. The native worksheet here creates and removes its own database.</p>
<p>In the initial capture, the common report took 144.217 ms; the rare report took 0.543 ms. Those isolated observations identify a parameter-sensitive difference, not a service latency distribution. The next evidence should explain what work differs.</p>

<h2 id="follow-where-the-report-spends-its-work">Follow where the report spends its work</h2>
<p>A plan is a tree of physical data dependencies. A parent consumes rows from its children. Follow the most indented inputs towards their consumers, while remembering that consumers decide how many rows to request. The tree is not a timeline of SQL clauses.</p>
<p>This is the common report’s before plan, shortened and relabelled from the native capture. Costs, widths and most execution details are omitted here so the dependencies remain legible.</p>
<pre class="a26-plan"><code>{before}</code></pre>
<p>The customer <strong>sequential scan</strong> tests the table’s visible rows and rejects other regions. For each survivor, the <strong>nested loop</strong> requests matching orders. Their <strong>bitmap index scan</strong> collects locations; the <strong>bitmap heap scan</strong> visits the base rows in page order, checks any necessary index rechecks and applies the dates. All dates pass here. No rows were removed by index recheck, and all bitmap heap blocks were exact, rather than lossy page-level candidates.</p>
<p>The next nested loop probes the line key for each order. An ordinary <strong>index scan</strong> follows matching entries to base rows; a condition under <code>Filter</code> would reject rows after that access. <code>Index Cond</code> does not prove that every named condition narrowly bounds a scan. <a href="/review/relational-next/indexes/">Choosing indexes for a workload</a> owns key order and eligibility; <a href="/review/relational-next/physical-storage/">How stored rows become physical work</a> owns heap and cache mechanisms.</p>
<p>Play Figure 1 for north, then switch to island. Watch the number of requests made to each inner input.</p>
<PlanFigure scene="flow" number={1} />
<p><strong>Hash aggregation</strong> accumulates totals by customer, consuming the qualifying lines before this final ranking can finish. The top-N sort retains the best ten, but still examines all 8,000 totals. Its ten emitted rows do not mean it processed ten inputs. Returning a short result has not bounded the underlying report.</p>
<p>The captured aggregation used one batch and about 3.3 MB; the top-N sort used 25 kB. Neither spilled. In another capture, <code>Sort Method: external merge</code>, disk usage, hash batches above one or temporary block traffic would identify additional work. A spill alone establishes neither a statistics fault nor that a global memory increase is safe. Per-operation memory and concurrent demand matter together.</p>
<p>Different join methods can implement the same matching rule. A <strong>hash join</strong> first builds a lookup structure from one input, then probes it with rows from the other. A <strong>merge join</strong> advances through inputs ordered by their join keys, handling equal-key groups together. Their setup, ordering and memory costs can earn their keep when repeated probes become numerous. We will observe the hash alternative after testing the estimate.</p>

<h2 id="interpret-counts-costs-and-timings-before-blaming-a-node">Interpret counts, costs and timings before blaming a node</h2>
<p>This line scan comes from the separate warm capture with per-node timing enabled. Its text form is reconstructed from the retained JSON fields, using PostgreSQL 18’s decimal row display.</p>
<pre class="a26-plan"><code>{node}</code></pre>
<p><code>rows=3</code> predicts output per invocation, not every entry inspected. <code>width=13</code> predicts average output bytes per row. The cost pair prices startup and complete consumption in planner units, not milliseconds. Startup is the work before the first row can be emitted; total assumes all output is requested. A consumer such as <code>LIMIT</code> can stop earlier.</p>
<p><code>actual rows=3.00 loops=80000</code> reports average emitted rows per execution and the number of executions. That is 240,000 emitted lines here. Display rounding limits multiplication in less regular data: PostgreSQL 18 shows decimal row averages, while rows-removed fields are rounded whole-number averages. Do not interpret a displayed zero as proof that no row was ever rejected.</p>
<PlanFigure scene="units" number={2} />
<p><code>actual time</code> reports average startup and total elapsed milliseconds per loop, including child work. The tiny displayed line timings are rounded; multiplying them gives an approximation, not a precise total. Never add inclusive node times or subtract them to claim exclusive CPU time. The containing line join’s 119.164 ms already includes its 29.764 ms order subtree.</p>
<p>Buffer counters also include children, but are already accumulated across loops. The line scan’s 480,000 shared hits must not be multiplied again. A hit is an access satisfied in PostgreSQL’s cache. A read loads a block into that cache, possibly from the operating system’s cache; it is not necessarily device I/O. Counters count accesses, not distinct pages. Dirtied blocks became dirty during the work; written blocks were written out during it, possibly carrying earlier changes. Neither measures commit durability. Temporary blocks describe intermediate files, separately from table and index buffers.</p>
<p>Use the top node once for a whole-plan buffer comparison. The warm timed before capture reports 576,098 shared hits and zero reads. Its initial untimed capture reported 575,839 hits and 259 reads: the same total accesses under a different cache condition. Do not manufacture an I/O speed ratio from those counts.</p>
<p>Per-node clock reads add overhead, especially for many tiny probes. <code>TIMING OFF</code> keeps row and buffer evidence and overall execution time, but loses each node’s startup and total times. Optional <code>track_io_timing</code>, when enabled for an authorised collection, adds I/O duration evidence at its own measurement cost. Planning and execution reports still exclude application connection wait, network transfer and application work; ordinary EXPLAIN does not send the result rows to the client.</p>
<p>Before comparing estimated and observed output, ask whether the node ran and whether its input was consumed sufficiently. An early-stopped ordered scan may emit ten of a correctly estimated thousand. A never-executed branch provides no observed selectivity. Our hash aggregation consumes all qualifying lines, so its upstream row counts support this comparison. No parallel nodes occur in these captures; their worker accounting would need a separate reading, because overlapping worker intervals cannot be added into elapsed time.</p>

<h2 id="locate-the-first-error-that-could-change-the-choice">Locate the first error that could change the choice</h2>
<p>The line lookup predicts three rows and emits three. Its problem is that it runs 80,000 times. Work backwards: those requests come from 80,000 orders, which come from 8,000 customers. The customer scan predicts 18. This is the earliest observed mismatch that can explain why repeated lookup appeared attractive.</p>
<p>A simplified model makes the multiplication visible. At 0.1% north customers, <code>10,000 × 0.001 = 10</code> customers lead to <code>10 × 10 × 3 = 300</code> line contributions. At 80%, the same arithmetic produces <code>8,000 × 10 × 3 = 240,000</code>. This is illustrative arithmetic, not PostgreSQL’s estimator. The native scan estimates 18 and the line join 303; physical-size scaling and join estimation mean its intermediate predictions need not equal this simple product.</p>
<p>Predicted cardinality affects the alternatives priced by the planner: repeated probes, join order, anticipated row-processing work and the sizes of sorts and hashes. Width and expected page traffic matter as well. Estimates influence costing; they do not directly allocate a fixed amount of memory per predicted row. The largest error ratio in the tree is not automatically the place where a correction would change a useful decision.</p>
<p>State a falsifiable explanation: the region distribution changed after statistics collection, so PostgreSQL underprices the number of probes. A targeted refresh should raise the customer estimate and may replace repeated lookup with broader scans and hash joins. Inspect the summary and current data before testing that change. If estimates were already accurate, the same expensive work could be necessary; runtime contention or imperfect costing would be different explanations.</p>

<h2 id="check-the-statistics-behind-that-prediction">Check the statistics behind that prediction</h2>
<p>Inspect the implicated table rather than refreshing the entire database on suspicion. On this bounded fixture, a full grouped count is inexpensive enough to compare with the summaries. On a large service table, that diagnostic scan itself needs a budget; use existing distribution evidence or a bounded observation first.</p>
<Sql code={stats} label="Inspect the prediction, compare the bounded data, then test one refresh" />
<p>Before the refresh, <code>pg_stats</code> still assigns north frequency 0.001. The actual grouped count reports 8,000. <code>pg_class</code> retains 10,000 estimated tuples and 55 pages from collection; the updated heap now has 98 pages. PostgreSQL scales relation estimates against physical size, which helps explain why the scan predicts 18 rather than ten. None of those catalogues is a continuously exact row counter.</p>
<p>The column summaries answer different questions. <code>null_frac</code> estimates the null fraction. Positive <code>n_distinct</code> estimates distinct values; a negative value expresses a fraction of the row count, with −1 indicating uniqueness. Common values and their frequencies represent skew directly. Histograms describe the remaining non-null distribution after common values are excluded. This three-value region column has all values in its common-value list and no histogram; customer identifier has a histogram and no common-value list.</p>
<p>Single-column summaries cannot express every combination. In this fixture, every island customer has country I. Fresh individual frequencies are both 0.001. Treating those conditions as independent suggests <code>10,000 × 0.001 × 0.001 = 0.01</code> matching rows; a captured equality query estimates one and emits ten. The dependency is between two attributes on the same row. The separate <code>correlation</code> field in <code>pg_stats</code> concerns value order versus physical row order for access costing, not this logical dependency.</p>
<p>Freshness, summary capacity and unrepresented dependency are different faults:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Statistics hypotheses and tests"><table><thead><tr><th>Evidence</th><th>Next targeted test</th><th>Cost or limit</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Known drift; old common-value frequency</td><td><code>ANALYZE customers</code>, as here</td><td>Collection reads and processing; continued collection as data changes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fresh sample misses consequential irregular skew</td><td>Raise that column’s statistics target and recollect</td><td>Larger sample and summaries, more collection and planning work</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fresh individual summaries miss a same-table combination</td><td>Supported extended statistics, then <code>ANALYZE</code></td><td>Dependency statistics support selected constant equality and IN forms, not arbitrary ranges or column-to-column comparisons</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><code>CREATE STATISTICS ... (dependencies)</code> or a multicolumn common-value summary can address appropriate same-table predicates. Creating the object alone does not collect data. PostgreSQL 18 does not use extended statistics to correct cross-table join selectivity. Read its <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/planner-stats.html#PLANNER-STATS-EXTENDED">supported forms</a> before proposing that remedy. The report has no country predicate, so the isolated change chosen here remains the targeted refresh.</p>
<PlanFigure scene="estimates" number={3} />
<p>After <code>ANALYZE customers</code>, north frequency becomes 0.8 and the customer estimate becomes 8,000. The plan builds hashes and scans orders and lines once, then aggregates the qualifying contributions. The common report still combines 240,000 lines and calculates 8,000 totals, but the 88,000 index searches disappear. Warm timed top-level hits fall from 576,098 to 2,550, with no temporary blocks in either capture.</p>
<p>The after plan’s top total cost rises from about 1,052 to 12,773. That is compatible with improvement: the old cost priced a job that was predicted to be much smaller. Costs across changed statistics do not measure a speed-up. The collection itself took 3.31 ms at the local client in this small run; larger tables and continued maintenance need their own budget. Fresh statistics remain approximate.</p>

<h2 id="check-the-application-s-common-and-rare-parameters">Check the application’s common and rare parameters</h2>
<p>A literal north query does not establish what a reused application statement will do. The worksheet uses <code>pg</code> 8.23.1 with a named query object on each physical connection. That driver path prepares once per connection and reuses the statement there. Other drivers may use unnamed execution, thresholds or other preparation rules; inspect the actual path rather than equating parameter binding with persistent preparation.</p>
<p>A PostgreSQL <strong>custom plan</strong> can use supplied parameter values. A <strong>generic plan</strong> is reusable across values and saves repeated planning, but must work without knowing this region. In <code>auto</code> mode PostgreSQL 18 first uses five custom plans, then compares their average estimated cost with a generic alternative, including the value of avoiding replanning. It does not switch automatically on execution six regardless of cost.</p>
<Sql code={prepared} label="Inspect the prepared statement in its own session" />
<p>Figure 4 deliberately forces both modes for bounded diagnosis, then resets the session. Compare custom and generic with island first; then compare north. Each pair executes the same SQL and parameter and returns identical results.</p>
<PlanFigure scene="parameters" number={4} />
<p>The rare custom plan estimates ten customers and uses indexed probes: 818 top-level shared hits, 0.512 ms execution and 0.167 ms planning in that capture. The rare generic plan estimates 3,333 customers, scans both larger inputs and records 2,550 hits, 16.225 ms execution and 0.106 ms planning. Planning savings do not pay for its extra work here. Common custom and generic both choose hashes, but different join orders and individual timings do not establish a stable performance ranking.</p>
<p>The actual named-driver path in auto mode retained custom plans for all eight alternating common/rare executions after collection. The mixed trials below also retained ten custom and zero generic executions per connection in both phases. A generic-plan regression is therefore a demonstrated possibility, not the supported cause of this application’s original symptom. No cache-mode override is retained.</p>
<p>Prepared statements live in their server session. Updated statistics or relevant DDL can force re-analysis and replanning; a changed <code>search_path</code> can trigger reparsing. A reconnect or a different pool connection has its own preparation state. Check those conditions when comparing a fresh diagnostic session with a long-lived application session. If a locally scoped mode change becomes a candidate, compare both parameter classes including planning, document its narrow scope and define when to remove it.</p>

<h2 id="verify-the-remedy-and-close-the-investigation">Verify the remedy and close the investigation</h2>
<p>The supported cause is distribution drift before statistics collection caught up. The ordinary remedy is <code>ANALYZE customers</code> and an appropriate continuing collection policy. SQL, indexes and result semantics are unchanged. The same ordered identifiers and exact totals matched before and after for both regions and for the forced-mode comparisons.</p>
<p>A separate correctness fixture checked multiple orders, equal-valued lines that must both count, null prices and an all-null total, a null region, a missing order, tied totals and both date boundaries. It returned customers 1 and 2 at 20.00 each, followed by customer 3 with a null total. The identifier tie-break and explicit null placement make that order stable. A predicate or type rewrite would need the same checks before its performance mattered.</p>
<p>We then applied the parent’s comparison method in three short trials per phase. Two clients each completed 60 sequential operations: together 16 north reports, four island reports, 80 lookups and 20 revision-counter changes per trial. Each client waited for completion before sending its next request. Named preparation, parameter order and statement mix were the same. Measurements include the local driver round trip and decoding, but no HTTP handling or connection-pool wait.</p>
<PlanFigure scene="acceptance" number={5} />
<p>North p95 fell from 167.45–185.87 ms to 80.81–82.58 ms across trials, with all operations completing and no errors. Lookups remained comparable; change p95 ranges overlapped. The four island reports per trial are too few for a useful tail estimate. The removed searches, unchanged results and improved common-report latency support accepting the refresh for this fixture.</p>
<p>The evidence ledger is now complete: old frequency → underestimated customer input → excessive repeated probes; targeted collection → corrected estimate → hashes and scans → reduced buffer work and better common-report latency under the tested competition. The remaining 240,000 contributions are necessary for the requested totals. No index or memory change is needed to finish this diagnosis.</p>
<p>These trials are warm and short, with closed-loop clients that reduce offered demand when responses slow. They establish neither sustainable arrival capacity nor production tail latency. The updates model revision changes, not fulfilment; no comparison of CPU utilisation, device I/O, log rate or long maintenance cycles was collected. Before deployment, use the parent’s method with real parameter frequencies, wider intervals, representative writes and concurrency, measuring end-to-end useful completions and resource costs. <a href="/review/relational-next/concurrent-load/">Improving performance under concurrent load</a> owns a contention diagnosis when those observations disagree with isolated execution.</p>
<p>If accurate estimates still produce a surprising route, the planner may lack a useful legal alternative or price one imperfectly. A transaction-scoped planner control can test a specific alternative. A faster forced plan supports further investigation; it does not identify the cause by itself. Retain such a control only with a scoped rationale, a comparison across affected parameters and a removal condition. New index design belongs with <a href="/review/relational-next/indexes/">Choosing indexes for a workload</a>.</p>
<p>Recheck when a region becomes dominant, dates widen, per-customer order counts change, or prepared executions begin using a generic plan. Retain the plan, parameters, collection conditions and result checks beside the acceptance record. That makes the next surprising run a comparison against evidence.</p>
<p>To repeat the native evidence, <a href={worksheet} download="query-plans-verify.mjs">download the worksheet</a>. In a new directory, install Node.js 22 or later and <code>npm install --ignore-scripts pg@8.23.1</code>. Set <code>PG_URL</code> to an administrative connection on a disposable PostgreSQL server, then run <code>node query-plans-verify.mjs</code>. It needs database-creation privileges, creates a unique <code>rel3_a26_run*</code> database, writes <code>raw-results.json</code> beside the worksheet, closes its connections and drops that database. Supply your own connection when repeating elsewhere; its fallback is the local verification instance.</p>

